<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE V: </span>Accuracy of object detection &amp; instance segmentation on MS-COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and semantic segmentation on ADE20K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> for models in the UniNAS space. <math id="S4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AP}^{\text{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AP</mtext><mtext>b</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AP}^{\text{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AP}^{\text{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AP</mtext><mtext>m</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AP}^{\text{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S4.T5.14" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">mIoU</span> denote box and mask average precision and mean intersection over union, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image Classification.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we compare <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">UniNAS-A</span> to other networks in the UniNAS search space: EfficientNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, ResNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, CoAtNet and ViT with relative position bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. In order to compare the networks in a fair setting, we scaled the networks to the same number of stages and blocks, and by scaling the number of channels, we also adjusted for the same network size. Each network was trained on ImageNet-1k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, using the same training protocol as in <a href="#S3.T3" title="In Search step. ‣ III Architecture Search ‣ Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, the training took 2 days on 8 A100 GPUs. As shown in <a href="#S4.T4" title="In Architecture search. ‣ IV Results ‣ Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">UniNAS-A</span> outperforms standard hand-crafted networks by a great margin.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06035v1/UniNAS_small.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/463;" width="429" height="463"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>UniNAS-A architecture. The architecture resembles two heads of a self-attention mechanism, but upon closer inspection, certain operations are added or removed.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream Tasks.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then fine-tune each network from the previous step on two downstream tasks – object detection on MS-COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and semantic segmentation on ADE20K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, using identical settings from <a href="#S3.T3" title="In Search step. ‣ III Architecture Search ‣ Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>. Training required approximately 10 hours for segmentation and 21 hours for detection<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
            To adapt the model to different input sizes, we interpolated the relative position bias module without any other architectural modifications. ViT was excluded from downstream evaluation, as it did not fit to GPU memory with the larger image resolution required by the downstream tasks.</span></span></span> on 4 A100 GPUs. Once again, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">UniNAS-A</span> significantly outperforms existing networks (see <a href="#S4.T5" title="In Architecture search. ‣ IV Results ‣ Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proxy Ablation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we ablate the choice of VKDNW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> as our proxy, by running the search <a href="#alg1" title="In Search step. ‣ III Architecture Search ‣ Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with different proxies, and then evaluating the best architecture found by each proxy. In <a href="#S4.T6" title="In Proxy Ablation. ‣ IV Results ‣ Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> we show that the search through the UniNAS space remains mostly robust to the choice of a specific proxy, but VKDNW still being able to find the best network architecture under identical search budget (12 hours).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
